ing done so that left us with an  opportunity to really become much closer  to that 25% goal so what have we learned  well first increasing the scope of  testing only increases actionable  aberrations slightly we need more  effective drugs and it's important that  we have more drugs available for our  clinical trials and indeed we make a  mistake of not counting that whole  population of patients who go on  standard of care drugs we only were  looking at those that went on clinical  trials and this markedly underestimated  the value of our treatment program we  also needed to have a process to deal  with these rare actionable advanced  across tumors and we set up a series of  bucket  to deal with these things such as a K T  and T R K we also found that there is a  challenge with tumor heterogeneity and  therapy induced evolution and I'm going  to talk about that later one of the big  things though and probably the biggest  of the whole process is that many of our  physicians were not comfortable using  the information that was being provided  from the molecular testing and so we  have set up a molecular tumor board a  clinical trial alert that tells the  patient the physician that their patient  has a clinical trial that matches with  their aberrations and also have  established a major decision support  program that I'm going to talk about in  more detail in a few moments I've  already mentioned these other points one  important one to bring forward is that  testing itself attracts high quality  information rich trials in patients and  institutions should consider whether  they want to consider the cost of  testing a loss leader to increase their  market penetrants and to bring in trials  that are going to be beneficial for  their patients so one of the things that  we did do is go in and ask what was  happening in terms of patient testing if  we added this standard of care that is  moving patients to drugs that had  already been approved but needed genomic  testing for entry and indeed what we  found was that other patients that went  on trials we had only been focusing on  this clinical trial group which was at  58% but we found that 36% of our  patients with positive results did  receive standard of care for drugs that  were effective and surprising to us a  number of patients received drugs  off-label and likely benefit from that  use so actually the benefit on our  studies were much higher than we had  predicted from our initial analysis now  one of the key challenges that we have  is as we move into an era of targeted  therapy and molecular tests  it is impossible for anyone to  understand the complete complexity and  all of the potential opportunities that  arise as one test patients so we have  had to build a medical decision support  program and as I mentioned earlier it is  one of the major reasons why we increase  the success of our program one of the  biggest challenges that we have in this  decision support is that there are many  aberrations that we find and we do not  know whether they are drivers or  passengers that is a driver is a genomic  event that causes the tumor to behave  inappropriately at the time the patient  presents and the passenger is those that  happen due to genomic instability so we  have developed a major program that we  feed into our physician decision support  program in which we characterize several  hundred mutations in particular genes or  uncle genes each month for their  functional effects and what we would do  in terms of targeting those  abnormalities and this has been funded  by the CT d squared program of the  National Cancer Institute so we  integrate that with our information this  shows you where the problem is we have  over a million aberrations that have  been identified in patients tumors and  under a thousand of them have been  functionally annotated so we have this  major problem of many or most of the  mutations that we identify in patients  we do not know whether they are going to  be a driver or passenger there are now  over 20 different algorithms or programs  to attempt to identify whether a new  aberration is a driver or a passenger  and simply stating that there are 20  different approaches indicates that none  of them work well so this functional  annotation is critical and one of the  comments that I noted earlier is that  some mutations are very important and  this is that idh1 and ID h2 mutation and  it changes the function of the gene  because of that you have to change the  particular drug that you use  this is a very important concept in that  if you were to use a drug targeting the  parental idh1 and only partially block  idh1 you may actually make the outcome  of a patient with an idh1 mutation worse  because it is a different functional  mechanism than that of the parental  molecule these are called neo morphs and  are going to be critical to make this  happen as I mentioned earlier we have  set up a broad-based program to identify  the characteristics of these aberrations  and we make this information available  to our faculty in real time we do not  use this to manage particular patients  in front of us but rather to develop a  catalog of aberrations that we can use  to manage patients throughout their  course of care now our personalized  cancer website is identified here and  you can find it just by looking at  personalized cancer therapy and the MD  Anderson Cancer Center or you can look  pull it up from https PCT MD Anderson  org we have annotated fully 27 genes and  that is over 90% of all of the  aberrations that one will find in cancer  that's due to the fact that we annotated  all of the commonly mutated genes and  left out those very long tails we've  annotated them for all abnormalities  that we can detect the physician is able  to determine the level of information  that they want going anywhere from you  should treat this patient with a  particular drug on a trial going down to  the paper that says why this is the case  we also very importantly provide a level  of evidence for each recommendation that  we suggest we also provide where  clinical trials are available with a  heavy emphasis on making sure that all  of the trials available to MD Anderson  Cancer Center X date now one of the  things that we've done with that  decision support program is to ask  whether that will improve the number of  patient that goes on clinical  miles misses our most recent trial where  we tested patience deeply approximately  400 patients were entered on the trial  and just over half of them had a variant  in an actionable gene and importantly we  reached our goal of 25% with mutations  and actionable genes being enrolled on  clinical trials we actually may be doing  much better than that in that at least  12% of our patients have not proceeded  throughout their clinical care far  enough where they would be eligible for  entry on to those trials so we do expect  those numbers to improve and we were a  little bit disappointed in that we had a  number of abnormalities in patients  tumors where we did not think these were  drivers we call them variants of unknown  significance and many physicians still  enrolled them on clinical trials as if  these were driver aberrations and indeed  when we queried our physicians they said  that those trials were as useful or  likely to be useful as any other trial  that was available to a patient and it  certainly was not likely to cause bones  or harm the exciting observation is that  despite the fact that this was a very  early trial we saw a marked benefit for  the patients who had a matching drug any  matching therapy the line in blue at the  bottom is those patients who did not  have an abnormality or did not have a  match drug and they did not do well but  those at the top of had an abnormality  and a match therapy did much better and  indeed in a Phase one program to see  over half of the patients still alive at  18 months is truly a homerun and a  remarkable outcome that should be  convincing payers that this is indeed  beneficial to our patients now one of  the problems as we go forward in  broad-based testing is that although we  test the patient's tumor we find  information that is present in their  germline and inheritable germline  aberrations not only influence the  patient's in front of us but influence  their family  and so what are we going to do with this  particular information what if you find  an abnormality in brca1 or brca2 or p53  where this has a marked impact and what  will happen on it on their family also  some others that are really low risk  variants or rare variants such as those  in FGFR where again it is not clear what  you would do with that information so we  tested germline variants in a thousand  patients and found that 99% of those  patients requested return of results  even though these results can influence  their family quite broadly and they were  concerned about their own cancer they  wanted to know what was going on that  could have contributed to development of  their cancer we thought that this might  be a major problem finding many  aberrations that would overwhelm our  genetic counselors who help patients  deal with their family and family  history it turned out that that was not  the case in the 19 genes that are  recommended for testing by the clinical  testing groups we found 43 germline  mutations and a thousand patients with  advanced cancer 19 or sorry 20 of those  had already been identified and the  patient knew they were in their family  most of those were brca1 and brca2  breast cancer woman breast cancer 2 here  in red one very big surprising  observation that we had were  abnormalities in p53 p53 is the cause of  the lead from any syndrome and we  thought it was highly penetrant however  it turned out we identified a number of  individuals who had inherited  abnormalities in p53 in the same  location as those in the leaf for many  syndrome and they had not developed any  evidence of a leaf remaining syndrome in  their family we have now worked with  colleagues from multiple centers and  have found that this is indeed a general  observation and we are trying to  understand what this means in terms of  the impact of p53 mutations in the pace  Jeremih now one of the challenges that  we have moving forward is that although  our patients respond and those with a  biomarker benefit the responses are  usually short-term and that's because  resistance is almost universal and one  of the biggest concerns that has come  forward in the last several years is  that heterogeneity will be a major  challenge that will prevent us from  fulfilling the promise of personalized  cancer medicine if we look at where  mutations happen in a tumor those that  are in solid markers at the top of the  pathways are frequently mutated whereas  those towards the bottom of the pathway  are rarely mutated indeed if we look at  a functionally vantur a systems biology  concept almost all of the mutations that  we find at the surface of cells or in  proteins at the surface of cells  integrate to a very limited number of  downstream effectors pathways and that  means that it is possible that  convergent evolution or activation of  similar pathways may allow us to deal  with the heterogeneity that is present  in tumors we explore this extensively by  studying what is happening in proteins  and protein signaling and draya Califano  has a major programme looking at RNA as  a readout for what is happening in  protein pathways now this is the  original paper in the New England  Journal of Medicine that suggested that  intramural heterogeneity was going to be  a major problem in targeted therapy and  personalized medicine and indeed when  this paper came out many of my  colleagues and patients said that this  could be the death knell for targeted  therapy altogether and that there were  so many different variations in both the  primary tumor and then the metastasis  and what you can see here in blue are  all of the in gray and blue or all of  the different mutations that are present  in different regions of primary  metastatic to  what has they looked at in detail my  auntie katrell and others that were part  of this original paper is that tumors  appear to have a trunk and branch format  you can see on both of these renal cell  carcinomas that were presented in those  two paper that there is a major trunk in  blue which has an abnormality in pH L  which is common in renal cell carcinoma  you also see that all of the metastases  present in those batches have different  mutations and there are indeed many  differences in the metastasis from the  primary tumor however when we look at  this more closely there's an important  observation that I think was missed in  the original paper and is actually very  exciting in terms of the potential  success of targeted therapies so what  you find is is that as one looks at each  one of these branches they have a common  set of aberrations the patient on the  left in every branch has an abnormality  in set E - with an error arrow in red or  in a very similar or related molecule  KDM SC in blue what happened here was  three separate branches of evolution in  this tumor acquired different  abnormalities in set B - suggesting that  once you had a VHL mutation there was an  incredibly powerful Selective pressure  to bring in a set d2 mutation for this  tumor to progress and as we look at the  tumor on the right side we see the same  thing happening the HL on the trunk and  a set d2 mutation different one in each  case on every branch of the tumor and  further similar abnormalities in p10 or  the pi3-kinase pathway in multiple  branches once again indicating that  there is a very strong selective  pressure for these abnormalities to  happen  we've indeed gone in and looked deeply  at patients with breast can  answer with primer in metastasis then  find that over 85% of the time there are  no significant abnormalities that are  targetable between the different primary  and metastasis no do note that doesn't  mean there aren't many other aberrations  but rather that those driver events that  I pointed out earlier are common across  most of the tumor this 85 percent  concordance means that about 15 percent  of tumors will have different ever  aberrations in the primary in the  metastasis and it is important to  understand that this is a study from  another group that looked at primary and  recurrent breast cancers and asked what  were the difference and remarkable in  those particular cases the only  significant aberration that they found  in these tumors was an increased  frequency of mutations in the metastasis  of estrogen receptor mutations indeed  all of these patients had received  estrogen receptor targeted therapy and  that was the driver that selected and  probably caused the aberrations in the  estrogen receptor and indeed go back to  the comment that I made early on that  lineage specific targets are likely to  be high quality targets now this leaves  us with a problem of having to try and  understand what is happening not only in  the primary tumor in the patient but in  the metastasis and it is impossible to  biopsy every metastasis that is present  in a patient one of the most exciting  observations of the last several years  in terms of targeted therapy is that  liquid biopsies have come to the fore  where we can look at circulating DNA DNA  that is released from the tumor into the  circulation and capture most of the  information that is present in terms of  mutations throughout the body indeed  this integrates all of the information  from the primary and the metastatic  tumors we've done studies here at the MD  Anderson Cancer Center headed by Philip  Janko and  found a remarkable concordance between  what you find in the circulating DNA and  in the solid tumor with over 85 87  percent concordance and this is what we  would expect with the heterogeneity that  I mentioned earlier of 85% of lesions  having the same abnormality and 15  percent having discordant C's so we  think this is an accurate representation  of what is going on and indeed we are  moving that into our clinical management  of patients you can detect actionable  aberrations in metastatic cancer you can  monitor response to ask if the mutation  pattern is changing with therapy you can  look for acquired resistance mechanisms  by the targeted therapy and it may be  better than looking at the primary tumor  because you captured all of the  metastatic events across the body there  are opportunities in early detection all  those those are very premature at this  point in time it is exciting to consider  the opportunity to monitor response to  therapy and also determining whether a  patient has benefited markedly from that  therapy allowing us to either stop  therapy if they're doing extremely well  or change therapy if a new aberration is  arising I'd like to thank the people in  my laboratory that have contributed to  the work on the left hand side and to  reinstitute for molecular markers or the  center for molecular markers and the  Institute for personalized cancer  therapy on the right side and people  that we collaborate from around the  world to make these opportunity possible  for our patients thank you thank you dr.  Mills for that informative presentation  we will now start the live Q&amp;A portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the green Q&amp;A button at the  lower left of the presentation window  type your question into the box that  appears on your screen and click on the  send button we'll answer as many  questions as we have  for so let's get started our first  question is dr. Mills you've argued that  it's going to be difficult to deliver  personalized molecular medicine what are  the key next steps in this process the  most important next steps in this  process is to have a better pattern or  better set of effective drugs so far our  targeted therapies have been used  primarily as single agents mono therapy  and we have learned over generations of  experience that targeted their single  therapies are likely or not very likely  to be effective in complex diseases such  as epithelial cancers indeed we've  needed to combine chemo radiation  multiple chemotherapy agents to have  marked responses in our patients with  epithelial tumors therefore one of the  key next steps is going to be the  development of rationale combinations of  targeted therapies that overcome  resistance that seems to be inevitable  for most of our patients great thank you  so much for that answer we're going to  wrap up with one more question and that  question is does personalized medicine  include immuno oncology to take the  concepts here that all types of  therapies that we use for our patients  need to be better personalized even the  chemotherapy and radiation therapy that  we use need to be targeted to those  individuals who are most likely to  benefit and that will then justify the  toxicity of those particular therapies  the same applies as we move into the era  of targeted therapy and immunotherapy we  should be thinking of treating our  patients with the best combination of  all of these agents for that particular  individual indeed we already know that  several of our target  therapy's potentiate the effects of  immunotherapy and immunotherapy  potentiates the effects of a number of  our targeted therapies so we need to  think of a concept of therapy and not  focus on whether this is a particular  modality and use the appropriate  combination of radiation chemo surgery  and immunotherapy and targeted therapies  for each individual that we see in the  clinic thank you thank you dr. Mills do  you have any final thoughts appreciate  your listening to the talk I think that  we have an incredible opportunity to  help our patients and that this is going  to be one of the very exciting eras that  we've had where we have more  opportunities more drugs and more  chances to make a difference for  individuals than we have ever had before  thank you for your attention I would  like to once again thank dr. Mills for  his presentation I would also like to  thank leverage for making today's  educational webcast possible before we  go I want to let everyone know that  today's webcast will be available for  on-demand viewing through August of 2017  you will receive an email from Lavery's  letting you know when this webcast will  be available for replay please share  that announcement with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us we hope to see you again soon goodbye  you
 

